    "question": "In early Mizo tradition, to adopt the religion of another clan is called-",
    "options": [
      "Sakung",
      "Sedawi",
      "Saphun",
      "Sekhuan"
    ],
    "explanation": "Saphun was the ritual by which a person abandoned the sakhua of his own clan and took on the religion of another clan, involving a sacrifice performed by a priest of the adopting clan. It mattered socially because sakhua defined clan identity and the obligations that came with it, so saphun effectively transferred a household's religious allegiance. Sakung, the main house-post associated with clan sacrifices, is the near-miss distractor.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
