    "topicLabel": "Indian Polity & Governance",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "A party is recognized as a state party in a state if: (i) It secures 6% of the valid votes polled in the state at a general election to the legislative Assembly of the state concerned (ii) It secures 4% of the valid votes polled in the state at a general election of the Lok Sabha from the state concerned (iii) It wins 3% of seats in the legislative assembly at a general election to the legislative Assembly of the state concerned (iv) It wins 1 seat in the Lok Sabha for every 25 seats or any fraction thereof allotted to the state at a general election to the Lok Sabha from the state concerned Select the correct answer using the codes given below:",
    "options": [
      "(i), (ii) and (iii) only",
      "(i), (iii) and (iv) only",
      "(ii), (iii) and (iv) only",
      "(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"
    ],
    "explanation": "A state party generally needs 6% valid votes in the state Assembly poll (with 2 seats), or wins at least 3% of Assembly seats, or wins 1 Lok Sabha seat per 25 (or part) allotted to the state; the vote-share figure in statement (ii) reads as 4% rather than the actual 6%, making (i)+(iii)+(iv) the better-fitting combination.\n\n💡 It's like a coupon rulebook where one clause has the wrong percentage printed on it — everything else checks out except that one number.\n\n⚠️ Statement (ii)'s '4%' sounds official and specific, which is exactly what makes a subtly wrong number convincing.",
