    "topicLabel": "Indian Economy",
    "difficulty": "easy",
    "question": "Fiscal policy in India comprises of the following components: (i) Government receipts (ii) Government expenditure (iii) Public debt Select the correct answer using the codes given below:",
    "options": [
      "(i) only",
      "(ii) only",
      "(i) and (ii) only",
      "(i), (ii) and (iii)"
    ],
    "explanation": "Fiscal policy is the use of the government's budget, and all three listed items are its components: receipts on the revenue side including taxes, expenditure on the spending side, and public debt as the borrowing that bridges the gap. Since the deficit is financed by borrowing, debt management cannot be separated out from fiscal policy, which is why any option excluding item (iii) fails. Contrast this with monetary policy, which the RBI runs through the repo rate, CRR, SLR and open market operations.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
