    "topicLabel": "Mizoram GK",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "‘Oilzapper’, developed by TERI (The Energy and Resources Institute), is a notable Indian innovation for bioremediation. It specifically consists of a consortium of bacteria highly effective in degrading:",
    "options": [
      "E-waste and heavy metals.",
      "Crude oil and oily sludge (hydrocarbon waste).",
      "Plastic and polymer waste.",
      "Agricultural and municipal solid waste."
    ],
    "explanation": "Oilzapper is a bacterial consortium developed by TERI that breaks down spilled crude oil and oily sludge into harmless byproducts like water and CO2.\n\n💡 Tiny bacterial \"clean-up crews\" treat spilled oil like their favorite meal, munching through the greasy sludge until it's gone.\n\n⚠️ \"Plastic and polymer waste\" is tempting since bioremediation is often linked to plastic-eating bacteria in the news, but Oilzapper targets hydrocarbon oil waste.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
