    "topicLabel": "Mizoram GK",
    "difficulty": "medium",
    "question": "ISRO’s successful development of high-thrust cryogenic engine technology, crucial for its GSLV Mk-III (LVM3) launch vehicle, involved overcoming the extreme challenge of handling:",
    "options": [
      "Liquid hydrogen at -253°C and liquid oxygen at -183°C.",
      "Refined kerosene (RP-1) and solid aluminum propellants.",
      "Unsymmetrical dimethylhydrazine (UDMH) and nitrogen tetroxide.",
      "Monomethylhydrazine (MMH) under hypergolic conditions."
    ],
    "explanation": "Cryogenic engines like GSLV Mk-III's burn liquid hydrogen (~-253°C) and liquid oxygen (~-183°C); the huge challenge is storing and handling these super-cold propellants safely.\n\n💡 It's like carrying ice-cream so cold it would freeze and shatter your hands the moment you touched it.\n\n⚠️ \"UDMH and nitrogen tetroxide\" is tempting since it's real Indian rocket fuel too, but that's a storable hypergolic propellant, not cryogenic.",
    "source": "MPSC State Tax Officer / Group B Gazetted",
